<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Reconciliation" cic="CCC 1420-1498" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>Name at least four names of the sacrament of Reconciliation and explain what each one emphasizes.</li>
<li>In which biblical passage does Christ institute this sacrament? Cite the key verse.</li>
<li>List the three acts of the penitent and explain each one in two lines.</li>
<li>What is the difference between perfect contrition and imperfect contrition? Which of the two suffices for a valid confession?</li>
<li>What does it mean that confession must be "integral"? How is a mortal sin confessed?</li>
<li>What is the sacramental seal? Is there any exception?</li>
<li>What are the effects of the sacrament of Reconciliation? Name at least four.</li>
<li>What is an indulgence and what is required to gain a plenary indulgence?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question with total honesty. Write by hand if you can.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. When was your last confession? If it was more than six months ago, what has stopped you from going back: shame, laziness, fear, mistrust of the priest, the feeling that you have no serious sins?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be honest with yourself. Identifying the obstacle is the first step to overcoming it.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. In the parable of the prodigal son, the father runs toward the son before the son even finishes speaking. What does that tell you about what God is like? Have you ever experienced it?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe, if you can, a concrete moment when you felt God's unconditional forgiveness.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Is there any sin you have never confessed, or that you confessed incompletely, out of shame? Write here what kind it is (you don't need to detail it, just the category: against purity, against truth, against charity, etc.).</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. What is harder for you: asking for forgiveness or forgiving? Explain why.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Many people discover that forgiving someone who hurt them is even harder than recognizing their own mistakes.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Write the name of a person you need to forgive. Then write why you haven't forgiven them yet. What would you lose if you forgave them? What would you gain?</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">6. If you knew with absolute certainty that the priest does not judge you, that he forgets your sins immediately, and that he is utterly incapable of telling anyone under any circumstances &mdash;would anything change in how you confess?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Sometimes it's not faith that fails; it's human trust.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Guided Examination of Conscience</h4>
<p>Review the Ten Commandments. Next to each one, write whether you have kept it well, moderately, or poorly in the last month. For those you mark "poorly," jot down briefly what happened. You don't have to show this to anyone; it's for you. But it will help you in your next confession.</p>
<ol>
<li>Love God above all things: ___________</li>
<li>Do not take God's name in vain: ___________</li>
<li>Keep holy the Lord's Day: ___________</li>
<li>Honor your father and mother: ___________</li>
<li>Do not kill: ___________</li>
<li>Do not commit impure acts: ___________</li>
<li>Do not steal: ___________</li>
<li>Do not lie or bear false witness: ___________</li>
<li>Do not consent to impure thoughts or desires: ___________</li>
<li>Do not covet the goods of others: ___________</li>
</ol>

<h4>B. Preparing Your Next Confession</h4>
<p>Write on a separate sheet (that you can bring to the confessional):</p>
<ol>
<li>How long it has been since your last confession.</li>
<li>Mortal sins in kind and approximate number.</li>
<li>The venial sins that are most repeated.</li>
<li>Any circumstance that changes the species of the sin (if any, briefly).</li>
<li>Something for which you want to thank God (there's always something).</li>
</ol>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Meditative Reading of the Parable of the Prodigal Son</h4>
<p>Read Luke 15:11-32 in full. Now underline or note down every action the father performs. Count them. How many acts of love did you find? Compare: how many words does the son say and how many actions does the father do? What does that tell you about how God's love works?</p>

<h4>D. Rembrandt's Father's Hands</h4>
<p>Find Rembrandt's <em>The Return of the Prodigal Son</em>. Study it closely. The father's hands are different: one is more masculine and strong (like a father's hand), the other is more delicate and fine (like a mother's hand). Rembrandt meant that God loves us with both fatherly and motherly love. Write down what that detail suggests to you. Have you experienced God as Father, as Mother, or neither?</p>

<h4>E. Your Personal Act of Contrition</h4>
<p>Compose your own Act of Contrition in your own words. Don't copy the traditional formula. Make it yours. Start: "Jesus, I am sorry for... because..." It can be brief. But let it be sincere.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>F. Reconciliation Commitment</h4>
<p>Write a concrete commitment for this week:</p>
<ul>
<li>I will go to confession. Date: ___________ Church/time: ___________</li>
<li>I will forgive ___________ with a concrete gesture: ___________</li>
<li>I will ask forgiveness from ___________ for ___________</li>
</ul>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Verse to Memorize</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;Receive the Holy Spirit.<br />
If you forgive the sins of any,<br />
they are forgiven;<br />
if you retain the sins of any,<br />
they are retained.&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; John 20:22-23</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, Good Shepherd,<br />
who said to the paralytic: "Your sins are forgiven,"<br />
and to the good thief: "Today you will be with me in Paradise":<br />
give me the humility to acknowledge my faults without excuses,<br />
the courage to confess them without hiding anything,<br />
and the gratitude to live in your forgiveness<br />
without falling back into guilt.<br />
Give me also a heart that knows how to forgive<br />
as you forgive me:<br />
without conditions, without holding grudges,<br />
with the joy of the father who runs to meet his son.<br />
May I never forget that your mercy<br />
is infinitely greater than all my sins.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

